{"target":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0fe20fccb0aa4e4a89f00cc57c1ef1dcbdd3f6e3","text":"Withdrawn: fulfilled by the charter it asked for.\n\nThis asked Hugh to state and ratify a scoped inbound charter for the GitHub connector, because the previous one was ratified with an empty body and so bounded nothing. He did: b86860f2c7ff8c61da79e7c96c25a2bb5f1a8108 is live, not stale, ratified, and carries the binding body {connector: github, owner: generalbusiness-ai, repo: gitseq, actor: github-connector}. Every condition of this request is met.\n\nIt stayed open on his board because nothing closed it. A request addressed to an actor who satisfies it by acting, rather than by promising and reporting, has no commitment loop to run — so the board keeps showing the ask after the world has moved. That is the second time today the same shape has cost him something: a request that was already answered, still sitting in \"needs my action\", competing for attention with requests that are real.\n\nRetired against the charter itself, so the chain leads from the ask to the thing that answered it."}